>
    

    <article class="story">
      <p>I'm at the dusty Nairobi bus station, buying a ticket to see my favourite aunt, four hours away. One day, she packed her bags and moved to the village by herself. Said she was done with the family drama and the fast city life, and went off-grid.</p>

      <p>She's the bravest person I know.</p>

      <p>I get my ticket and board the bus. I'm one of the first passengers on, so I score my favourite seat: one row ahead of the back, by the window. I settle in and people-watch, praying that a weirdo doesn't sit next to me. Or a talker.</p>

      <p>The bus fills up pretty quickly. A lady sits beside me, holding a drowsy toddler. I hope she's not the fussy kind.</p>

      <p>A cute girl about my age smiles at me as she slips into the window seat behind me. She smells nice. I barely get a good look at her before the conductor yells, “One more, one more! Twende!”</p>

      <p>And a guy stumbles in.</p>

      <p>Bloodshot eyes. Unkempt hair. Clothes that look like they've been slept in for several days. As he passes me to sit behind us, I catch a whiff of his unwashed body, with a hint of piss and stale beer.</p>

      <p>He plops down next to the cute girl and belches.</p>

      <p>She immediately cracks the window open.</p>

      <p>The bus roars to life. We're past the CBD, and it's midmorning. The highway is clear.</p>

      <p>I'm restless, bored already. My legs won't keep still. I brought books, a Nat Geo, and...</p>

      <p>I glance at the fruit salad in my bag. I made it this morning. Generous chunks of mango, pineapple, and banana, with a squeeze of lemon. And I packed about ten grams of Golden Teacher.</p>

      <p>What if I eat some?</p>

      <p>I wasn't planning on tripping, but this is four hours of <em>please don't let me suffocate.</em></p>

      <p>I pull out the salad. The smell of ripe mango hits my nostrils.</p>

      <p>Fuck it.</p>

      <p>I sprinkle about half a gram onto a chunk and start eating. It tastes like earth and sugar. I put my earphones on and listen to flute instrumentals, waiting for the gentle nudge of the mushroom to flow over me.</p>

      <p>The unsightly man behind us starts talking to the girl. I can hear him over my earphones.</p>

      <p>Slurred speech. “Hey, beautiful. Where are you going? You got a boyfriend?”</p>

      <p>She says nothing.</p>

      <p>The volume on my earphones is all the way down now because he's making a racket. I take a deep breath and look behind me.</p>

      <p>A thick, grey cloud is hovering over him. The girl is visibly disgusted and irritated.</p>

      <p>I immediately turn back to my phone. I know the mushrooms are kicking in, and I'm slightly nervous.</p>

      <p>Minutes later, she snaps.</p>

      <p>“DRIVER, STOP THE BUS!”</p>

      <p>The whole bus jerks. Brakes screech. The conductor drags the dark-cloud man out by the collar while he continues yelling.</p>

      <p>As the door closes, the cloud goes with him.</p>

      <p>Sucked right out.</p>

      <p>Suddenly, the windows are bigger, and I can breathe.</p>

      <p>I turn up the volume, take out my Nat Geo magazine, and start browsing.</p>

      <p>Page fourteen.</p>

      <p>I stop at a waterfall in the Amazon that I cannot seem to turn away from. The water is moving on the page. The ink smells like rain, and when I blink, mist hits my face.</p>

      <p>The lady and toddler beside me are fast asleep. I stare at the page for almost half an hour.</p>

      <p>We stop for a bathroom break. Most people are jolted awake, and everyone piles out.</p>

      <p>I don't need to go.</p>

      <p>The baby's mother notices, looks at me, and asks desperately, “Can you... just two minutes?”</p>

      <p>I nod, and she hands me this warm, glowing ball of joy.</p>

      <p>About one year old.</p>

      <p>I am holding a human.</p>

      <p>What should I do? Pet it?</p>

      <p>The baby hiccups and grins. We stare at each other. She notices my Nat Geo, points at the waterfall, and giggles.</p>

      <p>Her mum comes back, but we're engrossed in the sand dunes of Namibia, the sound of wildebeest at a waterhole, and the cerise colour of the sun dying behind Kilimanjaro. The sharp, acidic smell of Prunus africana trees. The breezy rainforest in Peru.</p>

      <p>Her mother lets me hold her all the way until we arrive. When she finally takes her back, she stretches in relief.</p>

      <div class="ending">
        <p>My aunt is waiting at the stage with two sodas.</p>

        <p>She takes one look at me and knows.</p>

        <p>“Road trip?” she asks.</p>

        <p>“Road trip,” I say.</p>

        <p>She slings an arm around me, and we walk to her car.</p>
      </div>
